---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { project, directionSource } from '../data/project';
const adr = (name: string) => `${directionSource.replace('/tree/', '/blob/')}/docs/adr/${name}.md`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Security — Vox Lucis" description="Who can read your messages in Vox Lucis, how the post-quantum encryption works, and what no encryption can protect." path="/security/">
  <header class="section-pad page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SECURITY</p><h1>You decide who reads you.<br /><em>Nobody else does.</em></h1><p class="lede">Only the people in your keyring read your messages. The encryption pairs classical and post-quantum algorithms. This page also says plainly what Vox cannot protect.</p></header>
  <div class="section-pad docs-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Security topics"><p>SECURITY</p><a href="#consent">Your keyring</a><a href="#services">Shared services</a><a href="#cryptography">Encryption</a><a href="#infrastructure">No central server</a><a href="#boundaries">What it cannot protect</a></nav>
    <div class="prose">
      <section id="consent"><h2>Your keyring decides who reads you</h2><p>Your node hands your keys only to the nodes in your keyring, and takes keys only from them. Joining a room grants nothing. The other side decides for itself, so a two-way conversation needs trust both ways.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Trust covers every room you share.</strong> Trusting Ann applies to the rooms you share now and later, and to the services you share in them. Nobody else trusts Ann because you do.</p></div><p>Each entry grants <strong>read</strong>, or <strong>read + drive</strong>, which also lets that node drive your agents’ Sessions. Every keyring change asks for your passphrase, typed at a terminal or into the Mac app’s prompt, which names the change it approves.</p><p>When someone joins a room you share, Vox offers them to your keyring. You accept, naming them, or dismiss them without telling them. Names are your own aliases for fingerprints, so compare the full fingerprint over a channel you trust before you accept.</p><p>New trust starts with messages from then on, unless you grant your full history. Removing trust changes your keys, so the removed node reads nothing new. What it already read stays read.</p><p><a href={adr('ADR-020-agent-comms')}>Keyring design</a></p></section>
      <section id="services"><h2>A room gives context. Trust gives access.</h2><p>A node shares a named service, such as SSH or a local web app, with a room. When a member connects, the host checks both its keyring and the room’s membership. An invitation alone reaches nothing.</p><p>You reach a service as <code>service.node.room.vox</code>, in your own names for the node and the room. Stop sharing it or withdraw trust, and its live connections end.</p></section>
      <section id="cryptography"><h2>Classical and post-quantum, together</h2><p>Every key exchange and signature uses two algorithms at once, one classical and one post-quantum, so an attacker must break both.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Vox cryptographic algorithms"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Classical</th><th scope="col">Post-quantum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Key agreement</td><td>X25519</td><td>ML-KEM-768</td></tr><tr><td>Signatures</td><td>Ed25519</td><td>ML-DSA-65</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Each room message is sealed under its sender’s key. Each new key travels to each trusted member in a fresh post-quantum session of its own, so someone who once stole a node’s keys, even with a quantum computer, cannot read the keys that come after.</p><p><a href={adr('ADR-003-post-quantum-and-crypto-agility-policy')}>Cryptographic policy</a> · <a href={adr('ADR-030-pq-post-compromise-security-for-pairwise-key-delivery')}>Post-quantum key delivery</a></p></section>
      <section id="infrastructure"><h2>No central server</h2><p>Rooms live on their members’ devices. When two devices cannot reach each other directly, an anchor run by you or someone you know helps them connect, and relays encrypted traffic if it must. An anchor holds no room keys and reads nothing.</p><p>Members who are never online at the same time still get each other’s messages through a member who overlaps with both.</p><p><a href={adr('ADR-012-nat-traversal-and-reachability')}>Reachability design</a></p></section>
      <section id="boundaries"><h2>What Vox cannot protect</h2><ul><li><strong>A copy someone already has.</strong> Retention clears messages from cooperating devices. It cannot erase a screenshot.</li><li><strong>A compromised device.</strong> Malware or a keylogger on a device reads what its owner reads.</li><li><strong>Who talks to whom, and when.</strong> The network still sees traffic timing and volume. Vox does not promise anonymity.</li><li><strong>Delivery while offline.</strong> Messages wait until a path between members opens.</li><li><strong>Read receipts.</strong> A connection does not prove anyone read a message, and Vox never says it does.</li></ul><p>Before you rely on Vox for something critical, read the threat model and the source. <a href={adr('ADR-001-vox-foundation-vision-threat-model-and-principles')}>The full threat model</a> · <a href={project.repository}>The source</a></p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
